<h1>How 0Stakes Tests Casino Strategies</h1>

<p>Every simulation-backed article in <a href="/the-pit/">The Pit</a> follows the same ground rules. This page states them once instead of repeating them in every article.</p>

<h2>What every simulation does</h2>
<ul>
  <li><b>Mathematically correct game rules.</b> Simulations use the same payouts, dealer rules, and odds as the real game they're modeling, not simplified stand-ins.</li>
  <li><b>Stated trial counts.</b> Every article states how many hands, rolls, or spins were simulated, and any fixed random seed used, so the run is reproducible.</li>
  <li><b>Documented assumptions.</b> Bankroll, bet size, table limits, deck counts, and rule variations used in a given experiment are spelled out before the results.</li>
  <li><b>Unbiased random outcomes.</b> Simulated dice, cards, and wheel spins draw from the same true probabilities as the real game, not weighted or adjusted to produce a particular result.</li>
  <li><b>Checked against known math.</b> Where a theoretical probability or house edge is already established (a wheel's house edge, a hand's basic-strategy edge), simulation output is checked against it before publishing.</li>
</ul>

<h2>What the results mean, and don't mean</h2>
<p>Large simulations illustrate long-run behavior, the outcome a strategy converges toward given enough trials. They are not a prediction of what happens in any individual session. A result that holds over a million hands can still look completely different over the fifty hands anyone actually plays in an evening.</p>

<p>No betting strategy on this site is presented as removing a negative expected value unless the underlying math itself changes, for example, an Odds bet in craps priced at true odds instead of a house-favored payout. Changing bet size, timing, or sequencing changes volatility and how losses arrive; it doesn't change the game's built-in edge.</p>

